# Generated agent scaffolding. `openspec update` recreates all of it, so none of
# it carries project knowledge and all of it churns on a CLI upgrade.
# .github/ is NOT ignored wholesale — CI workflows belong in the repository.
.claude/
.github/prompts/
.github/skills/
.opencode

.pytest_cache
.specstory
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# Note: `data/*`, not `data/**`. A trailing /** excludes the directory itself, and
# git cannot re-include a file through an excluded parent — which would make the
# negation below inert. data/* matches the children directly, so the one file we
# want can be named back.
data/*
data_test/*

# ...except the alias table, which is configuration rather than derived data:
# links.load_project_cache reads it at runtime to map a moved identity onto its
# current one, so a clone without it strands every cache directory that moved.
# Regenerate with scripts/build_project_aliases.py.
!data/project_aliases.json

# ...and the human-verified portal links, which are the same kind of thing: a decision
# recorded once and needed again. Not regenerable, because regenerating them would mean
# re-deriving a person's reading of a portal page from nothing.
!data/twur_overrides.json

# data/projects.previous.json stays ignored. It is derived: the 8/11 baseline
# re-ingested from source.pdf, which is tracked, so it is reproducible with
#     python -m urtpe.cli source.pdf -o <tmp> --no-archive
# rather than 2.8 MB of regenerable data in the history.

# The completion corpus is derived from the archived gazettes, which are read-only
# inputs outside the repository. Rebuild with `urtpe.corpus.build_corpus`, which
# caches per archive member; without the cache a run rescans every archived PDF,
# which is most of a rebuild's wall time.
.corpus_cache/

# docs/temp.* is a paste pad, not a document: in-flight questions, audit output
# and draft rules copied out of the viewer mid-investigation. It is pinned to one
# moment -- the counters, the harvest PID and the unresolved-project lists in it
# were true of a single run -- so it is stale the moment it is written and states
# no project knowledge that a reader can act on later. Keep it locally; do not
# let it accumulate in history. Anything worth keeping belongs in docs/ proper
# or in openspec/.
docs/temp.*
